## 1. [1000. Minimum Cost to Merge Stones](https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-cost-to-merge-stones/)

### Solution

```java
class Solution {
    public int mergeStones(int[] stones, int k) {
        int n = stones.length;
        if ((n - 1) % (k - 1) != 0) {
            return -1;
        }

        int[] prefixSum = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            prefixSum[i + 1] = prefixSum[i] + stones[i];
        }

        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int len = k; len <= n; len++) {
            for (int i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
                int j = i + len - 1;
                dp[i][j] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
                for (int m = i; m < j; m += k - 1) {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], dp[i][m] + dp[m + 1][j]);
                }
                if ((j - i) % (k - 1) == 0) {
                    dp[i][j] += prefixSum[j + 1] - prefixSum[i];
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[0][n - 1];
    }
}
```

### Time and Space Complexity

- Time Complexity: O(n^3)
- Space Complexity: O(n^2)

### Intuition

- The problem can be solved using dynamic programming.
- We can define a 2D array `dp` where `dp[i][j]` represents the minimum cost to merge stones from index `i` to index `j`.
- We can iterate over the length of the subarray and split it into smaller subarrays of length `k`.
- We can use the prefix sum array to calculate the sum of stones in a subarray.
- We can use the formula `dp[i][j] = min(dp[i][m] + dp[m + 1][j]) + prefixSum[j + 1] - prefixSum[i]` to calculate the minimum cost to merge stones from index `i` to index `j`.
- We can return `dp[0][n - 1]` as the answer.

